    crm:P129i_is_subject_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/diary/1899-02/document/61> ;
    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/493296565/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """on these hills. I found it oppressive. They were both shy, shy, graceless – the type of English to strike horror into a foreign heart – yet I expect very good, intelligent girls, and one might like them quite well, getting to know them. But I felt no desire to go on. 
Bernhard went to the Uffizi with Count Stroganoff. We dined at the Rosses, and had a bottle of Johannisberger – divine! Lina’s uncle, Canon Waterton, a dear, simple old Catholic priest, was there. We walked back through the woods – bright moonlight and nightingales. 
Thursday May 25.99.
Miss Lowndes and Mr. Benn came to lunch. Bernhard walked with Benn and called on the Kerr-Lawsons. I worked upon Sogliani. In the evening we went over the chapter of Bernhard’s book on Filippino, Garbo, and Carli. An  awfully  windy day. """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 61 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1899-02> .

